  "prompt_text": "The figure uses a river landscape as a visual metaphor for biological development or cell-state progression. A stream begins high in the mountains at a “source,” representing changes in progenitors, then flows downhill through branching tributaries that mark different axes of maturation. As the river descends, it follows a broad developmental path while encountering both internal and external influences that alter its course.\n\nIntrinsic factors are depicted as waterfalls and rapids in the upper terrain, suggesting built-in constraints or transitions within the system. Extrinsic factors appear later as dams and eddies, indicating environmental influences that can slow, redirect, or trap progression. One prominent pool off the main channel represents “diversion,” a nonphysiological state where the flow leaves the normal trajectory. A side channel lower down suggests a maturation shortcut, allowing some paths to bypass part of the usual route.\n\nAcross the whole landscape, the figure conveys development as a continuous but branching and perturbable process rather than a single straight line. The downhill slope and labeled gradient imply increasing differentiation, with species or cell-type differences shaping how trajectories vary along the way.",
